# properpin's two lines for /etc/pam.d/kde, shown around the stock "auth substack password-auth"
# line they go just above and just below; the stock line itself stays as it is. Only
# packaging/integrate-into-pam.sh add puts them in (just integrate-into-pam), after a diff and a
# question; install.sh never touches /etc/pam.d.
#
# crates/pam/tests/stack.rs builds its PAM stack from this very file, swapping only the module's
# path and the stock substack, so the control columns tested are the ones that would ship. The
# container test (just podman) puts them into a stock Fedora for real, through the same script.
#
#   check         the PIN: a match ends the stack with success, never sooner than the config's
#                 min_milliseconds_before_pin_unlock after the pinchecker started (kscreenlocker 6.8
#                 takes a faster answer for a broken authenticator); anything else is ignored at
#                 once, and the stock stack checks the same input as the password, faillock and all
#   housekeeping  forgives the user's own recent failures, the password among them, and unpauses
#                 the PIN, after a password unlock. pin_unpause_by in /etc/properpin/config says
#                 how it knows: password-check (the default) runs after the stock stack whatever it
#                 answered, since a failed substack doesn't stop the stack, and the pinchecker
#                 checks the password again through unix_chkpwd; setcred waits for pam_setcred,
#                 which libpam replays down to this line only after the stock stack succeeded
auth        sufficient    /usr/local/lib64/security/pam_properpin.so check
auth        substack      password-auth
auth        optional      /usr/local/lib64/security/pam_properpin.so housekeeping
